{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"The HVAC demand flexibility measures are **upgrades 24–28** in ResStock 2025 Release 1. They vary by **when the thermostat changes, the temperature offset, and the duration**. [Release upgrade list](https://resstock.nlr.gov/datasets)

| Upgrade | Strategy | Thermostat adjustment |
|---|---|---|
| **24** | On-peak load shedding, **2°F** | During the 4-hour peak window, lower the heating setpoint or raise the cooling setpoint by 2°F. |
| **25** | **4-hour** pre-peak load shifting, **2°F** | For 4 hours before the peak window, preheat by raising the heating setpoint or precool by lowering the cooling setpoint by 2°F. |
| **26** | On-peak load shedding, **4°F** | Same timing as 24, with a larger 4°F adjustment. |
| **27** | **4-hour** pre-peak load shifting, **4°F** | Same timing as 25, with a larger 4°F adjustment. |
| **28** | **1-hour** pre-peak load shifting, **2°F** | Same adjustment as 25, applied for only 1 hour before the peak window. |

These settings are documented in Table 8 of the [HVAC Load Flexibility report](https://docs.nlr.gov/docs/fy26osti/93846.pdf#page=33).

**Shedding** reduces conditioning during peak hours by allowing indoor temperatures to drift. **Shifting** conditions the building beforehand, using its thermal mass to reduce subsequent demand; the thermostat returns to its normal schedule during the peak window. The released measures do **not** combine preconditioning with an on-peak setback. Larger offsets and longer preconditioning generally provide greater flexibility. [Measure methodology](https://docs.nlr.gov/docs/fy26osti/93846.pdf)

One modeling caveat: these scenarios dispatch flexibility nearly every day to characterize technical potential. Their annual savings should therefore not be interpreted directly as savings from a typical program with occasional events. [Dispatch limitations](https://docs.nlr.gov/docs/fy26osti/93846.pdf#page=37)"}}
