{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"The HVAC demand flexibility measures are **upgrades 24–28**, corresponding to measure IDs **dr_001–dr_005**. They differ in whether they shed load during peak hours or shift it earlier, the thermostat offset, and the preconditioning duration. [ResStock release package list](https://resstock.nlr.gov/datasets)

| Upgrade | Measure ID | Strategy | Thermostat adjustment |
|---|---|---|---|
| **24** | dr_001 | On-peak load shedding, **2°F** | Lower heating / raise cooling setpoints by 2°F during the peak period. |
| **25** | dr_002 | **4-hour** pre-peak load shifting, **2°F** | Preheat / precool by 2°F for four hours before the peak. |
| **26** | dr_003 | On-peak load shedding, **4°F** | Lower heating / raise cooling setpoints by 4°F during the peak period. |
| **27** | dr_004 | **4-hour** pre-peak load shifting, **4°F** | Preheat / precool by 4°F for four hours before the peak. |
| **28** | dr_005 | **1-hour** pre-peak load shifting, **2°F** | Preheat / precool by 2°F for one hour before the peak. |

The upgrade IDs and offsets are documented in the [release README](https://oedi-data-lake.s3.amazonaws.com/nrel-pds-building-stock/end-use-load-profiles-for-us-building-stock/2025/resstock_amy2018_release_1/README_resstock_20251.pdf).

**Shedding versus shifting:** upgrades 24 and 26 reduce conditioning during the peak, allowing indoor temperatures to drift. Upgrades 25, 27, and 28 condition the home beforehand, using its thermal mass to reduce later demand; these scenarios have **no additional on-peak setpoint offset**. A 4°F offset is a larger intervention than 2°F, while four hours allows more preconditioning than one hour. [HVAC Load Flexibility documentation, Table 8](https://docs.nlr.gov/docs/fy26osti/93846.pdf#page=33)

There is a documentation discrepancy: the original README describes a **one-hour** shedding window, while the later measure documentation specifies **four hours**. The pre-peak durations above agree across both sources.

For analysis, these measures are dispatched nearly every day to expose technical potential. Use their daily profiles for selected dispatch days; the published annual savings should not be treated directly as savings from a typical demand response program. [Release modeling assumptions](https://oedi-data-lake.s3.amazonaws.com/nrel-pds-building-stock/end-use-load-profiles-for-us-building-stock/2025/resstock_amy2018_release_1/README_resstock_20251.pdf)"}}
