{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"The HVAC demand flexibility measures are **upgrades 24–28** (measure IDs **dr_001–dr_005**). They differ in **when the thermostat changes, the size of the temperature offset, and how long preconditioning lasts**. [Release package list](https://resstock.nlr.gov/datasets)

| Upgrade | Measure ID | Strategy | Thermostat adjustment |
|---|---|---|---|
| **24** | dr_001 | On-peak load shedding, **2°F** | Lower heating setpoint or raise cooling setpoint by 2°F during peak hours. |
| **25** | dr_002 | **4-hour** pre-peak load shifting, **2°F** | Preheat or precool by adjusting setpoints 2°F for four hours before the peak. |
| **26** | dr_003 | On-peak load shedding, **4°F** | Same strategy as 24, with a larger 4°F adjustment. |
| **27** | dr_004 | **4-hour** pre-peak load shifting, **4°F** | Same preconditioning window as 25, with a larger 4°F adjustment. |
| **28** | dr_005 | **1-hour** pre-peak load shifting, **2°F** | Same offset as 25, with a shorter, one-hour preconditioning window. |

The upgrade-to-measure-ID mapping is documented in the [release README, pp. 13–14](https://oedi-data-lake.s3.amazonaws.com/nrel-pds-building-stock/end-use-load-profiles-for-us-building-stock/2025/resstock_amy2018_release_1/README_resstock_20251.pdf).

**Shedding versus shifting:** Upgrades 24 and 26 reduce HVAC demand by allowing homes to become cooler during heating or warmer during cooling at peak times. Upgrades 25, 27, and 28 move heating or cooling earlier—raising the heating setpoint or lowering the cooling setpoint before the peak—so the building’s stored heat or coolness reduces subsequent demand. The final shifting scenarios have **no additional on-peak thermostat offset**. These controls apply to homes with heating or cooling, about 99.7% of the modeled stock. [HVAC Load Flexibility documentation, measure summary and Table 8](https://docs.nlr.gov/docs/fy26osti/93846.pdf)

For analysis, these scenarios run nearly every day to expose technical potential. Use their daily profiles for selected dispatch days; the release documentation advises against directly treating their annual totals as realistic program energy savings. [Release README, p. 7](https://oedi-data-lake.s3.amazonaws.com/nrel-pds-building-stock/end-use-load-profiles-for-us-building-stock/2025/resstock_amy2018_release_1/README_resstock_20251.pdf)"}}
